  "138": {
    "inputs": {
      "value": "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1> is 皇帝, a slightly full, upright man of about fifty-five with a broad oval face, high forehead, rounded jaw, pale blue-gray eyes, a small brown mole beneath the left eye, swept-back silver-gray hair, and a neat silver-gray beard and pointed goatee. <Picture 3> supplies identity only for <Subject 1>; current clothing comes from <Picture 1>.\n<Subject 2> is 宫廷总管, a lean, straight-shouldered man of about forty-eight with a long angular face, high forehead, pronounced cheekbones, narrow dark blue-gray eyes, a light brown mark at the right temple, slicked-back dark-gray hair, and a neat short goatee. <Picture 4> supplies identity only for <Subject 2>; current clothing comes from <Picture 1>.\n<Subject 3> is 骗子1, a tall, slender man of about thirty-five with a long narrow face, gray-green eyes, a small brown mole beside the right nostril, swept-back wavy dark-brown hair, and a tidy mustache. <Picture 5> supplies identity only for <Subject 3>; current clothing comes from <Picture 1>.\n<Subject 4> is 骗子2, a shorter, stockier but agile man of about thirty-two with a rounded-square face, amber-brown eyes, a small dark mole below the left corner of his mouth, warm light-tan skin, thick chestnut curls, and no beard. <Picture 6> supplies identity only for <Subject 4>; current clothing comes from <Picture 1>.\n\nsummary:\nA 16.25-second palace scene uses a mixed strategy: controlled same-axis movement preserves the hall's continuous geography, while brief speaker-motivated cuts secure readable faces and mouths during rapid exchanges. The Emperor's initial longing turns into anxiety as he confronts the possibility of being unable to see the cloth, walks to the throne, and sits in troubled thought.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Picture 1> guides the opening costumes, bodies, sunset-lit staging, established left-right order, and the four-character arrangement. <Picture 2> supplies the palace hall's architectural appearance. <Picture 7> identifies the two treadle looms, <Picture 8> supports the distant weaving-room light, and <Picture 9> identifies the high-backed throne. The identity images apply only to their bound Subjects and do not overwrite canonical costume, pose, floor position, blocking, or action. Preserve the Emperor's authorized path from the window toward the throne, the steward's respectful rear-right and later throne-side relation, and the two swindlers' fixed separation at their respective looms.\n\ndetailed_description:\nBegin from <Picture 1>: golden-red sunset fills the palace hall from <Picture 2>. <Subject 1> stands at the left foreground window with hands behind his back, gazing toward the illuminated weaving room. <Subject 2> waits respectfully behind and to his right. Deep beyond them, <Subject 3> and <Subject 4> remain separated at their respective treadle looms from <Picture 7>, making restrained continuous working motions. Preserve all established floor positions except for the explicitly assigned movement toward the throne. A modest same-axis camera adjustment gives <Subject 1> a useful profile-to-three-quarter view with his face and mouth readable while retaining the distant lit room. All other visible characters listen naturally with relaxed closed lips and no speech-like articulation. With the view settled, <Subject 1> (S1) says, <d>[Chinese] 我真想亲自去看看。</d>\n\nAs he finishes, <Subject 1> stops articulating and closes his lips naturally. Make a deliberate cut transferring emphasis from <Subject 1> to <Subject 2>, achieving a readable view of <Subject 2>'s face and mouth while he remains standing in his established rear-right position; <Subject 1>, <Subject 3>, and <Subject 4> listen or continue their authorized background work with relaxed closed lips and no speech-like articulation. With that view established, <Subject 2> (S2) answers, <d>[Chinese] 陛下若想去，现在就可以召他们准备。</d>\n\nWhen the answer ends, <Subject 2> relaxes his mouth and becomes an attentive listener. Cut from <Subject 2> back to <Subject 1>, securing a readable face and mouth as <Subject 1> completes one further step along the established window-to-throne path, stops with staggered feet, and tightens his brow toward the distant light; nobody changes sides or leaves the assigned blocking. <Subject 2>, <Subject 3>, and <Subject 4> remain natural closed-mouth listeners or silent workers with no speech-like articulation. Once the Emperor's troubled view is acquired, <Subject 1> (S1) says, <d>[Chinese] 若我看不见……</d>\n\n<Subject 1> ends the fragment, closes his lips, and turns his head and shoulders toward the steward without translating his feet. Cut from <Subject 1> to <Subject 2>, whose concerned face and mouth are clearly readable while he remains several steps behind-right and inclines his upper body slightly. <Subject 1>, <Subject 3>, and <Subject 4> keep relaxed closed lips with natural reactions and no speech-like articulation. With the steward's view established, <Subject 2> (S2) asks, <d>[Chinese] 陛下？</d>\n\n<Subject 2> finishes, closes his lips, and resumes restrained listening. Cut deliberately from <Subject 2> to <Subject 1>, obtaining a readable front three-quarter view of <Subject 1> while preserving their facing positions and the steward's distance; all other visible characters listen naturally with relaxed closed lips and no speech-like articulation. Once that view is secure, <Subject 1> (S1) says, <d>[Chinese] 若我看不见，岂不是显得愚蠢？</d>\n\nDuring and after this admission, <Subject 1> breaks eye contact, turns, and walks with controlled heavy steps along the established depth toward the high-backed throne from <Picture 9>. The camera travels on the same side of the axis, maintaining readable articulation without moving anyone merely for framing. <Subject 2> straightens, makes only the authorized small adjustment toward the throne's front side, yields the path, and stays respectfully oriented toward the Emperor. After a brief natural pause, the maintained view still clearly shows <Subject 1>'s face and mouth; <Subject 2> and every other visible character keep relaxed closed lips with no speech-like articulation. <Subject 1> (S1) continues, <d>[Chinese] 而且，我是这个国家的皇帝。</d>\n\nAs he speaks, <Subject 1> reaches the throne, turns, bends his knees, and sits securely. He leans slightly forward, lowers his gaze, presses one hand to an armrest, and rests the other index finger tensely at its edge. <Subject 2> stops several steps in front and to the side, while <Subject 3> and <Subject 4> remain small, separated workers in the luminous depth. Near the end, the camera begins only a very slow push; <Subject 1> shows merely the initial tendency to raise his head and slightly ease his finger, without straightening fully or reaching a brighter later expression. After speech ends, his lips close and the anxious seated arrangement settles naturally.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nRestrained palace-room tone with spacious natural reverberation; measured footsteps on the hard floor, subtle cloth movement, turns and weight shifts, then quiet seat compression and hand contact on the throne. From the distant weaving room come subdued wooden treadle rhythms and soft textile friction from two continuously operated looms, remaining subordinate because of distance. No extra human vocalization, narration, subtitles, or on-screen text.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nN/A"
